ther point already covered, but we need distinct lines. So we could have a sunny line through (2,1) and (1,1) (slope (1-1)/(2-1)=0, horizontal, not sunny). Through (2,1) and (1,2) slope = (2-1)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny. So we cannot have a sunny line covering (2,1) and any other required point because any line connecting (2,1) to (1,1) is horizontal, to (1,2) is slope -1. So any sunny line covering (2,1) must be a line that only covers (2,1) (i.e., passes through (2,1) and some other point not required). So we could have two distinct sunny lines both passing through (2,1) but with different slopes? They would intersect at (2,1) but be distinct lines. That's allowed: we can have two sunny lines both covering (2,1) (the same point can be covered by multiple lines). So we could have vertical line x=1 (non-sunny), sunny line L1: y = 2(x-2)+1 = 2x-3 (passes through (2,1) and (3,3) maybe not required), sunny line L2: y = 3(x-2)+1 = 3x-5 (passes through (2,1) and (3,4)). Both are sunny. So we have 3 lines, exactly 2 sunny lines, covering all required points: (1,1) and (1,2) covered by vertical line, (2,1) covered by both sunny lines (but at least one covers it). So k=2 works for n=3? Wait, the problem requires exactly k sunny lines among the n lines. So we have n=3 lines, k=2 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line. That seems to satisfy the conditions: all points are covered (vertical line covers (1,1) and (1,2); sunny lines cover (2,1) (both cover it). So k=2 works for n=3. But the solution says k=2 impossible for any n≥3. That seems contradictory. However, we need to check the condition: \"exactly k of the lines are sunny.\" So we need exactly k sunny lines. In our construction, we have 2 sunny lines, but we also have a non-sunny line. That's fine. But we need to ensure that the lines are distinct. The vertical line x=1 is distinct from the two sunny lines. The two sunny lines are distinct because they have different slopes. So we have 3 distinct lines. So k=2 works for n=3