lumn i contains points (i,b) where b ranges from 1 to n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points. So column n-2 has n+1-(n-2) = 3 points. Column n-1 has 2 points. Column n has 1 point. So indeed column n-2 has 3 points, column n-1 has 2 points, column n has 1 point.\n\nNow, we have n lines total, exactly k sunny lines. The non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or diagonal slope -1. The solution says: \"If k≤2, we need a vertical line for this column.\" Let's examine: If k ≤ 2, then we have at most 2 sunny lines, so at least n-2 non-sunny lines. The column n-2 has 3 points. A sunny line can intersect a column at at most 1 point? Actually a sunny line can intersect a vertical line (i.e., column) at at most one point, because a line not vertical can intersect a vertical line at exactly one point. So a sunny line can cover at most one point in a given column. Similarly, a diagonal line (slope -1) can intersect a column at at most one point (since it's not vertical). A horizontal line can intersect a column at at most one point (since it's not vertical). So any non-vertical line can intersect a given column at at most one point. So to cover 3 points in column n-2, we need at least 3 lines that intersect that column. If we have at most 2 sunny lines, we need at least 1 non-sunny line that is vertical (i.e., x = n-2) to cover the column's points. Actually we could also use a non-sunny line that is diagonal or horizontal, but those intersect the column at only one point each. So to cover 3 points, we need at least 3 lines intersecting that column. If we have at most 2 sunny lines, we need at least 1 vertical line (non-sunny) to cover the column's points. But we could also have a diagonal line that passes through two points in the same column? No, diagonal line slope -1 cannot be vertical, so it intersects a vertical line at exactly one point. So indeed to cover 3 points in a column, we need at least 3 lines intersecting that column. If we have at most 2 sunny lines, we need at least 1 vertical line (non-sunny) to intersect the column at more than one point? Actually a vertical line can intersect the column at all points (since it's the same column). So a vertical line x = n-2 can cover all 3 points. So we need at least one vertical line. So the solution says \"If k≤2, we need a vertical line for this column.\" That seems correct.\n\nNow, \"Analyzing columns x=n-1 (2 points) and x=n (1 point), we see that k=2 is impossible.\" Let's examine: If we have exactly k=2 sunny lines, then we have n-2 non-sunny lines. Among those non-sunny lines, we need at least one vertical line for column n-2. So we have at least one vertical line x=n-2. Then we have n-3 other non-sunny lines, which could be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. The columns n-1 and n have 2 and 1 points respectively. The solution claims that k=2 is impossible because the point (n,1) cannot be on a sunny line with points from column n-1. Let's examine that.\n\nColumn n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). The point (n,1) is the only point in column n. The solution says \"The point (n,1) cannot be on a sunny line with points from column n-1.\" Let's examine: Could we have a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and (n-1,1)? That line is horizontal (y=1), which is not sunny. Could we have a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and (n-1,2)? The slope is (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is slope -1, not sunny. So indeed any line through (n,1) and a point from column n-1 is either horizontal (if same y) or slope -1 (if connecting (n,1) to (n-1,2)). So those are non-sunny lines. So (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line that also covers a point from column n-1. So to cover (n,1) we need either a vertical line x=n (which would cover only (n,1) because column n has only one point), or a diagonal line x+y = n+1 (which passes through (n,1) and (n-1,2) and (n-2,3) etc). Actually diagonal x+y = n+1 passes through (n,1) and (n-1,2) and (n-2,3) etc. That diagonal is slope -1, not sunny. So it's non-sunny. So (n,1) can be covered by a non-sunny line: vertical x=n, diagonal x+y=n+1, or horizontal y=1 (non-sunny). So (n,1) can be covered by a non-sunny line. But the solution says \"The point (n,1) cannot be on a sunny line with points from column n-1.\" That's true. But does that imply k=2 impossible? Let's see.\n\nIf we have exactly k=2 sunny lines, we have n-2 non-sunny lines. We need to cover all points. We have a vertical line x=n-2 covering column n-2's 3 points. Then we need to cover column n-1's 2 points and column n's 1 point. We have n-3 other non-sunny lines left (since we used one vertical line). We also have 2 sunny lines left to use. The sunny lines cannot cover any point from column n-1 together with (n,1) because any line through (n,1) and a point from column n-1 is non-sunny. However, the sunny lines could cover points from column n-1 with other points not in column n. For example, a sunny line could pass through (n-1,1) and (n-2,2) maybe? Let's check slope: (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not sunny. So that fails. How about (n-1,1) and (n-3,3)? Slope = (3-1)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 2/(-2) = -1, again not sunny. Actually any line connecting (n-1,b) and (n-2,b+1) will have slope -1. So many points in the triangle are aligned on diagonals of slope -1. So any line connecting a point in column n-1 to a point in column n-2 will be slope -1 (since they satisfy a+b = constant). So those are non-sunny. So to cover points in column n-1 with sunny lines, we need to pair them with points not on the same diagonal. For example, (n-1,1) could be paired with (1,n-1)? That point is (1,n-1) which is in column 1, row n-1. The line connecting (n-1,1) and (1,n-1) has slope (n-1-1)/(1-(n-1)) = (n-2)/(-n+2) = -1. So again slope -1. So any point (i,j) and (i',j') with i+j = i'+j' will be on a diagonal slope -1. So any pair of points with same sum are on a diagonal. So to avoid slope -1, we need to pair points with different sums. For column n-1